1
00:00.500 --> 00:03.100 region:planner align:start
<v.host Interviewer>I&apos;m here with Lena Fischer,

2
00:03.100 --> 00:06.200
<v Interviewer>who plans the tram timetable for the city of Nordhavn.

3
00:06.200 --> 00:09.100
<v Interviewer>Lena, thanks for making time.

4
00:09.100 --> 00:12.500
<v Interviewer>Where does a schedule even begin?

5
00:17.500 --> 00:20.200 align:end
<v Lena Fischer>It begins with demand.

6
00:20.200 --> 00:22.800
<v Lena Fischer>On our busiest line,

7
00:22.800 --> 00:25.900
<v Lena Fischer><b>Line&nbsp;4</b> out to Kastellhoj,

8
00:25.900 --> 00:28.800
<v Lena Fischer>we run a tram every <b>6&nbsp;minutes</b> at the morning peak,

9
00:28.800 --> 00:32.200 line:-1
<v Lena Fischer>and then we stretch the headway back out to 12&nbsp;minutes

10
00:32.200 --> 00:34.900
<v Lena Fischer>once the rush is over.

11
00:39.900 --> 00:42.500
<v Interviewer>A tram every six minutes sounds relentless to plan for.

12
00:42.500 --> 00:45.600
<v Interviewer>How do you keep them evenly spaced?

13
00:50.600 --> 00:53.500 region:planner align:start
<v Lena Fischer>The enemy is bunching,

14
00:53.500 --> 00:56.900
<v Lena Fischer>where a small delay lets one tram fall back and

15
00:56.900 --> 00:59.600
<v Lena Fischer>catch the crowd meant for the next one.

16
00:59.600 --> 01:02.200
<v Lena Fischer>We fight it with a little timetable padding,

17
01:02.200 --> 01:05.300 align:end
<v Lena Fischer>a bit of slack built into the running time,

18
01:05.300 --> 01:08.200
<v Lena Fischer>and with a short turn at the <i>07:42</i> departure that

19
01:08.200 --> 01:11.600
<v Lena Fischer>turns back early at Vestport depot.

20
01:16.600 --> 01:19.300
<v Interviewer>What about the intersections?

21
01:19.300 --> 01:21.900 line:-1
<v Interviewer>The trams seem to sail straight through downtown.

22
01:26.900 --> 01:30.000
<v Lena Fischer>That is <i>signal&nbsp;priority</i>. Every tram carries a transponder that asks

23
01:30.000 --> 01:32.900
<v Lena Fischer>the traffic light ahead to hold its green a few

24
01:32.900 --> 01:36.300
<v Lena Fischer>extra seconds, and across the central corridor that alone saves

25
01:36.300 --> 01:39.000 region:planner align:start
<v Lena Fischer>us close to 90 seconds per trip.

26
01:44.000 --> 01:46.600
<v Interviewer>You mentioned the fleet earlier.

27
01:51.600 --> 01:54.700
<v Lena Fischer>Yes, the whole of <b>Line&nbsp;4</b> now runs the <b>CityRunner&nbsp;400</b>,

28
01:54.700 --> 01:57.600
<v Lena Fischer>a long articulated tram that bends in the middle and

29
01:57.600 --> 02:01.000 align:end
<v Lena Fischer>carries almost twice the passengers of the old cars.

30
02:01.000 --> 02:03.700
<v Lena Fischer>Higher capacity per tram is what lets us hold that

31
02:03.700 --> 02:06.300
<v Lena Fischer>six minute headway without flooding the street with vehicles.

32
02:11.300 --> 02:14.400
<v Interviewer>Is there any stretch of track that gives you trouble?

33
02:19.400 --> 02:22.300 line:-1
<v Lena Fischer>The <i>Bjornsson&nbsp;curve</i>, just south of the river,

34
02:22.300 --> 02:25.700
<v Lena Fischer>is a genuinely tight radius,

35
02:25.700 --> 02:28.400
<v Lena Fischer>so every tram has to crawl through it at walking

36
02:28.400 --> 02:31.000
<v Lena Fischer>pace. We simply write that slow section straight into the

37
02:31.000 --> 02:34.100 region:planner align:start
<v Lena Fischer>timetable rather than pretend the trams can take it at

38
02:34.100 --> 02:37.000
<v Lena Fischer>speed.

39
02:42.000 --> 02:45.400
<v Interviewer>How do you even know if the plan is working?

40
02:50.400 --> 02:53.100
<v Lena Fischer>We measure on time performance every single day,

41
02:53.100 --> 02:55.700 align:end
<v Lena Fischer>and right now <b>Line&nbsp;4</b> sits at about 92&#37;,

42
02:55.700 --> 02:58.800
<v Lena Fischer>meaning nine trams in ten arrive within our window.

43
02:58.800 --> 03:01.700
<v Lena Fischer>The other headache is <i>interlining</i>,

44
03:01.700 --> 03:05.100
<v Lena Fischer>where two routes share the same central tracks,

45
03:05.100 --> 03:07.800 line:-1
<v Lena Fischer>so a delay on one route quietly infects the other.

46
03:12.800 --> 03:15.400
<v Interviewer>Last question. How many people ride <b>Line&nbsp;4</b> on a normal

47
03:15.400 --> 03:18.500
<v Interviewer>day?

48
03:23.500 --> 03:26.400
<v Lena Fischer>On a typical weekday we count roughly 18,000&nbsp;boardings on <b>Line&nbsp;4</b>

49
03:26.400 --> 03:29.800 region:planner align:start
<v Lena Fischer>alone, and every one of those trips depends on a

50
03:29.800 --> 03:32.500
<v Lena Fischer>timetable that most riders will thankfully never have to think

51
03:32.500 --> 03:35.100
<v Lena Fischer>about.
